  standards to underpin  digital health passes she says there are  a few main features  every digital health pass should have  the first is something called  interoperability meaning it should work  across different institutions and  borders  a person can be you know tested or  vaccinated  wherever it is that they live and you  know get to  an airport or get to um you know  a border and have that past be  you know sort of seamlessly recognized  and trusted say you're traveling from  new york to london  a successful app would tell you the  latest guidelines for both cities  and integrate your official coveted  health records from each country  airlines are trying out different  options like common pass verifly  and iota travel pass but most trials are  limited to select routes  and passengers aren't required to use  the pass so it's hard to tell how it  would work on a mass scale  the reality is that so far you know  we're not really seeing this in a very  mandatory way  very many places because people don't  even know  how to mandate it and they don't know  what  counts as proof or evidence that you've  been vaccinated  also key to health passes according to  gruner are verifiable health records  these include things like vaccine  certificates as well as past coveted  test results in past cases  if you can have both proof of testing or  proof vaccination that allows people  sort of flexibility and sort of how  they if if they can't be vaccinated you  know perhaps they can be tested  in a digital health path system health  records would need to be uploaded to the  app and accessible to different  authorities  like physicians or border agents for  different uses  experts say consumers should be in  control of how much health data they  share  and with whom for example you might  choose to share more health information  with the doctor giving you your second  vaccine dose and less data with the  airline you're checking in with at the  airport  we think that this idea of selective  disclosure is really important  because it would allow me to share just  the minimum information  necessary when that information is  shared authorities need to know that  they can trust it  that's where another key factor comes in  verifiable credentials  and that is you know literally sort of a  packet of data  um that has it is verified um you know  that you can say  i this has been signed and sealed and i  sort of know that the  the packet of data has not been tampered  with and i know  who it was who's attesting to this  packet of data  but in order for digital health passes  to be embraced by both government and  regular people  they need to address the ongoing debate  over data privacy  in america privacy and health privacy  has always been a huge  hot button issue and people are  concerned  about sharing even that amount of  information and want to know that that  record is safe  some groups have been advocating for  centralizing health data since before  covid19  but resistance to sharing this data has  extended to the current pandemic  several states including texas have  banned having to show proof of  vaccination  including vaccine passports government  should not require any texan to show  proof of vaccination and reveal private  health information just to go about  their daily lives  cyber security experts in israel's  public health union have raised concerns  about sharing sensitive health data  this is a messy ecosystem there are a  huge number of potential  issuers of a health pass you know  testing centers  vaccinators states and also a huge  number of potential verifiers  um you know the airlines  you know border control agencies  workplaces and employers are also  considering tracking health records  one mercer survey found that almost 51  of companies are either currently  tracking their employees vaccination  status  or are considering it while individual  states are taking different approaches  to regulating vaccine passports  big tech is laying out some guidelines  for kovid related apps too  apple has said kovit 19 app developers  need to work with recognized government  or health authorities before submitting  to apple  and google has a list of additional  requirements for kovid 19 apps going  into its play store  still because there are so many apps out  there right now  the exact privacy policy could vary  slightly from each app  what's more some health officials  contend that tech companies are  over complicating the process and this  health information can be stored in a  much simpler form  like in a qr code and there are bigger  questions about how these apps will be  used together  some apps like the iata travelpass and  verifly are focused on the travel  industry  while others like israel's green pass or  new york's excelsior pass  are being used to grant people access to  events or spaces  the concern that essentially by asking  people to  prove that they've been vaccinated in  order to do very  you know normal things like go to work  or  attend a concert that we're creating a  system  in which there are two classes of people  the vaccinated and the unvaccinated  policy is as important as the technology  um and we need policy makers to define  the range of acceptable use cases  um you know and specifically you know  sort of  define what is not an acceptable use  case for a health pass  exactly where that line is is still  being worked out  and finding that balance is going to be  crucial for international travel  business and public health you
 

